    <p style="color:var(--muted);margin:12px 0 0;font-size:15px">The dish cable runs down to the router in the wheelhouse. The UCG Ultra feeds Switch 1 for the three 3rd deck APs and the crew PC, and one uplink cable drops to Switch 2 midships on the 2nd deck for the remaining three APs.</p>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="num">1</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Install the wheelhouse cabinet safely</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Keep the UPS and network gear at least 1–2 m from the magnetic compass.</li>
          <li>Use a closed, ventilated cabinet out of direct sun.</li>
          <li>Route network cables away from radar, GPS and VHF/GMDSS wiring. Don't share their power circuit.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="note verify"><span><b>Verify:</b> compass deviation is checked after installation.</span></div>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">2</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Pull spare Cat6 while cabling</h3>
        <p>Run one extra cable to a second ceiling position at each end of both bunk rooms and the reclining room. Bulkhead transits are the expensive part, so adding an AP later becomes a simple mount.</p>
      </div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- PHASE 03 -->
  <section class="phase" id="starlink" style="--c:var(--s4)">
    <div class="phase-head">
      <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 14a8 8 0 0 1 16 0"/><path d="M12 14v7"/><path d="M8 21h8"/><path d="M12 3v3"/></svg></div>
      <div><h2>Starlink</h2><p>Mount the dish, power the UTR-251 router, then hand routing to the UCG Ultra with bypass mode.</p></div>
      <span class="tag">Phase 03</span>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="num">1</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Mount the dish and power up</h3>
        <p>Bolt the rigid mount to structure on the compass deck centerline. Connect the dish cable to the router and power the router through the supplied UTP-253L power supply on the UPS.</p>
        <div class="note verify"><span><b>Verify:</b> the Starlink app shows online, and the obstruction map is clear in all directions.</span></div>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">2</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Connect the router to the UCG Ultra</h3>
        <p>The UTR-251 has two Ethernet ports. One carries the dish. Run Cat6 from the free port to the UCG Ultra <b>WAN (2.5G)</b> port.</p>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">3</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Enable bypass mode</h3>
        <p>In the Starlink app, open Settings and turn on Bypass Mode. The router's Wi-Fi switches off. To undo it later you must factory reset the router.</p>
        <div class="note verify"><span><b>Verify:</b> the UCG WAN shows a Starlink address, not <code>192.168.1.x</code>. A <code>192.168.1.x</code> address means bypass is not active (it still works, with double NAT).</span></div>
      </div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- PHASE 04 -->
  <section class="phase" id="gateway" style="--c:var(--s1)">
    <div class="phase-head">
      <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3" y="8" width="18" height="8" rx="2"/><path d="M7 12h.01M11 12h.01"/><path d="M15 12h3"/></svg></div>
      <div><h2>UCG Ultra and networks</h2><p>Adopt the gateway, update firmware, and split crew and passengers into separate VLANs.</p></div>
      <span class="tag">Phase 04</span>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="num">1</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Adopt and update</h3>
        <p>Adopt the UCG in the UniFi app or at unifi.ui.com. Set WAN to DHCP. Update UniFi OS and the Network application before configuring anything else.</p>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">2</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Create the networks</h3>
        <div class="tablebox" style="margin-top:4px"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Network</th><th>VLAN</th><th>Security</th><th>Portal</th><th>Users</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Management</td><td class="qty">1</td><td>—</td><td>No</td><td>UniFi devices only</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Crew</td><td class="qty">10</td><td>WPA2/WPA3 password</td><td>No</td><td>Crew phones, laptops, POS</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Guests</td><td class="qty">20</td><td>Open, type <i>Guest / Hotspot</i></td><td>Voucher</td><td>Passengers</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">3</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Wire the wheelhouse PC</h3>
        <p>The wheelhouse has no AP. Plug the PC into a free UCG Ultra LAN port with a normal Cat6 cable, then set that port's network to <b>Crew (VLAN 10)</b> in UniFi so it skips the voucher portal.</p>
        <div class="note verify"><span><b>Verify:</b> the PC gets a Crew VLAN address and browses without a login page.</span></div>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">4</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Secure remote access</h3>
        <p>Enable remote access so the network can be managed from shore. Protect the UI account with a strong password and two-factor authentication.</p>
        <div class="note verify"><span><b>Verify:</b> you can open the vessel's site from unifi.ui.com on a phone off the ship's network.</span></div>
      </div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- PHASE 05 -->
  <section class="phase" id="aps" style="--c:var(--s2)">
    <div class="phase-head">
      <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12.5a10 10 0 0 1 14 0"/><path d="M8.5 16a5 5 0 0 1 7 0"/><path d="M12 19.5h.01"/><path d="M2 9a15 15 0 0 1 20 0"/></svg></div>
      <div><h2>Switches and access points</h2><p>Two PoE switches, six APs, two SSIDs on every AP.</p></div>
      <span class="tag">Phase 05</span>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="num">1</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Connect and adopt both switches</h3>
        <p>Connect Switch 1 (wheelhouse) to UCG LAN port 1. Connect Switch 2 (2nd deck, midships) to Switch 1 through the uplink cable, using a non-PoE port on Switch 1 so its four PoE ports stay free for APs. Adopt both.</p>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">2</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Mount and adopt the APs</h3>
        <p>Plug each AP into its switch and adopt them one at a time. Name each by location so the crew can find it.</p>
        <div class="codebox"><pre>AP-D3-Canteen
AP-D3-Reclining
AP-D3-VIP-Hall
AP-D2-Bunk-Aft
AP-D2-Bunk-Fwd
AP-D2-Economy</pre><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">3</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Create the SSIDs and tune radios</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Crew SSID on VLAN 10, WPA2/WPA3.</li>
          <li>Passenger SSID on VLAN 20, open, Hotspot portal enabled, client isolation on.</li>
          <li>Channel width 20 MHz on 2.4 GHz and 40 MHz on 5 GHz. Lower transmit power on neighboring APs.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="note verify"><span><b>Verify:</b> all 6 APs show online. Each VIP suite gets usable signal with its door closed; add an in-wall AP to any suite that doesn't.</span></div>
      </div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- PHASE 06 -->
  <section class="phase" id="vouchers" style="--c:var(--s3)">
    <div class="phase-head">
      <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 7a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h14a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v3a2 2 0 0 0 0 4v3a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2v-3a2 2 0 0 0 0-4z"/><path d="M14 5v14" stroke-dasharray="2 2"/></svg></div>
      <div><h2>Voucher portal</h2><p>Branded login page, voucher packages, and the crew's selling procedure.</p></div>
      <span class="tag">Phase 06</span>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="num">1</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Enable the portal</h3>
        <p>Open Hotspot Manager, then Portal. Enable it on the Guests network and set authentication to <b>Voucher</b>.</p>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">2</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Brand the landing page</h3>
        <p>In the Landing Page editor add the logo, a vessel photo as background, terms of use, English and Filipino text, and a help note such as "Ask the purser for help." For full design control later, an on-board external portal server is an option.</p>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">3</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Create the voucher packages</h3>
        <p>Start with three and adjust after the first trips. Set prices against your Starlink data cost.</p>
        <div class="tablebox" style="margin-top:4px"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Duration</th><th>Devices</th><th>Down / up</th><th>Data cap</th><th>Price (PHP)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Quick</td><td>1 hour</td><td class="qty">1</td><td>2 / 1 Mbps</td><td>None</td><td>—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Day pass</td><td>24 hours</td><td class="qty">1</td><td>3 / 1 Mbps</td><td>500 MB</td><td>—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Trip pass</td><td>Full voyage</td><td class="qty">2</td><td>3 / 1 Mbps</td><td>2 GB</td><td>—</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">4</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Print and sell</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Print voucher batches before departure. Lock unsold slips with the cash box.</li>
          <li>Log every sale: code, package, time.</li>
          <li>Tell passengers: join the passenger Wi-Fi, wait for the login page, enter the code.</li>
          <li>No login page? Open any <code>http://</code> site, or turn Wi-Fi off and on.</li>
          <li>Everyone connected but nothing loads? Check the Starlink app before blaming vouchers.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="note verify"><span><b>Verify:</b> one test voucher works on a phone in every room.</span></div>
      </div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- PHASE 07 -->
  <section class="phase" id="security" style="--c:var(--s4)">
    <div class="phase-head">
      <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3l8 3v6c0 5-3.5 8-8 9-4.5-1-8-4-8-9V6z"/><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg></div>
      <div><h2>Security and bandwidth</h2><p>Keep passengers away from crew and equipment, and give each voucher a fair share of the link.</p></div>
      <span class="tag">Phase 07</span>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="num">1</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Block the Starlink dish from passengers</h3>
        <p>Add a firewall rule in the Policy Engine so the guest VLAN can't open the dish interface.</p>
        <div class="codebox"><pre>Action: Block
Source: Guests (VLAN 20)
Destination: 192.168.100.1/32</pre><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div>
        <div class="note verify"><span><b>Verify:</b> from a passenger phone, <code>http://192.168.100.1</code> and crew devices do not load.</span></div>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">2</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Apply caps and traffic rules</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Per-voucher speed cap of about 2–3 Mbps. At 100–200 Mbps of Starlink, that serves roughly 30–60 streaming users at once.</li>
          <li>Per-voucher data cap to protect per-GB ocean data.</li>
          <li>Traffic rules on Guests to limit video streaming, OS updates and P2P.</li>
        </ul>
      </div></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="num">3</span><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Handle hotspot sharing</h3>
        <p>Some Android phones can share the ship's Wi-Fi through their own hotspot. UniFi sees the whole group as one device, so the voucher's speed and data cap covers everyone behind it. Hard blocking needs TTL filtering, which UniFi doesn't offer.</p>
        <div class="note verify"><span><b>Verify:</b> share a 2 Mbps test voucher from an Android phone to a second device. Both speed tests together stay under 2 Mbps and count against one voucher.</span></div>
      </div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- PHASE 08 -->
